our stature  don't even acknowledge that it exists  how do you how do you deny our history  yeah in history that we're still living  out today with some of the politics of  today  the vote of suppression is basically a  lot of that is based on systemic racism  really is you detail in one of the most  powerful chapters in the book the  implicit bias training  that nypd officers go through and it is  truly impressive stuff  and yet you look at the record in the  city and there's been more than a  billion dollars  in payouts and settlements in line of  duty deaths where officers kill the  suspect  uh what do you make of that discrepancy  thank you for raising that we spent a  lot of money putting all 36 000 officers  through four days of training to make  them aware  of implicit bias but to actually do  something about it requires training  and a major focus of the book is that we  need to  refund police not defund that we're  going to need a lot more money to  retrain  to deal with bias to deal with the  homeless to deal with the emotionally  disturbed  commissioner one of the biggest sticking  points on federal police reform  is what's called qualified immunity and  holding officers accountable  what changes would you support to  qualified immunity  more importantly i think the police  profession police leadership are willing  to support  some changes in qualified immunity you  can't do away with it that  it is so critically important to the  morale of officers but also  in some respects of their livelihood but  it can be reformed  two key words dealing with qualified  immunity it's one  that the action has to be lawful in two  that the officers have to basically  uh be aware that what they're doing is  uh uh what a reasonable person would do  it's a very misunderstood  term and concept and i'm glad it's out  there being discussed  yeah i am too i i like that you said uh  implicit bias  is not a a is a human issue not a law  enforcement issue  but i think to follow up on anthony's  point most people  certainly people of color are upset  because it doesn't seem that police are  held accountable  most times in these situations you're  meeting people on your worst possible  day  the officers will say well don't resist  just do what we say they're trained to  maintain authority  we've got to figure out a way to see  each other in this in this time and we  don't do that  let's read alice harris to see each  other the good news about this is that  we have been reforming i talk about that  in the book that we've been reforming  for 50 years so we have more  accountability than we did  we have better training than we did  isn't that the issue or do you think the  issue is better training de-escalation  in particular they're the professionals  well i think  the training is the key to build trust  and it's essential to build trust  you're going to have to have better  training better accountability your  target  better transparency and we now have  capabilities there with the cameras that  they're all carrying no uh this is an  inflection point and let's  not get it long this time because we've  gotten so much wrong in the past  i'm very excited about the opportunities  out of the crisis of the moment well you  wanted to be a cop since you were a  toddler out there directing traffic  bought your first cop book at 11. you  said you love cops love being a cop but  you know there's work to do i know the  flaws yeah and the book is dedicated to  22  000 officers who have died in the line  of duty you say those cops count  police matter and if people are  interested in the police perspective  this certainly gives it to them  well thank you commissioner thank you  very much the book is the profession and  goes on sale tomorrow

